<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Orontes River</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Orontes is a river of the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>, flowing north through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>. The city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kadesh">Kadesh</a> stood on its banks. The Battle of Kadesh, fought there in 1274 BCE between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittite Empire</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-ii">Ramesses II</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, was one of the largest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a> battles in history.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/orontes-river.jpg" alt="Orontes River" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bernard Gagnon · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Noria_in_Hama_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Orontes, in Arabic Nahr al-ʿĀṣī, is the chief river of the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>. It rises in springs near Labweh in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a> on the east side of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beqaa-valley">Beqaa Valley</a>, between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-lebanon">Mount Lebanon</a> and the Anti-Lebanon Mountains, and runs north through a gorge. It then passes the Lake of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homs">Homs</a>, an artificial lake created by a Roman-era dam, and the cities of Homs and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hama">Hama</a> before entering the Ghab Plain, with the ancient city of Apamea on its eastern edge. Beyond Jisr al-Shughur it turns west into the plain of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hatay-province">Hatay Province</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, passes north of Antakya, the ancient Antioch, and enters the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> south of Samandağ. The river is not easily navigable, and its valley mattered as a road for north–south traffic.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>For the ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> the river marked the northern edge of Amurru, the region north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicia">Phoenicia</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age">Late Bronze Age</a> its valley held several important kingdoms and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-states</a>, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alalakh">Alalakh</a>, Niya and Tunip. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kadesh">Battle of Kadesh</a>, fought around 1274 BCE between the Egyptian army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-ii">Ramesses II</a> and the Hittite army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muwatalli-ii">Muwatalli II</a>, took place on the Orontes, as did the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-qarqar">Battle of Qarqar</a> in 853 BCE, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a>'s king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shalmaneser-iii">Shalmaneser III</a> met an allied army of twelve kings led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadadezer-of-damascus">Hadadezer of Damascus</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> acquired the valley after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-issus">Battle of Issus</a> in 333 BCE, and after his death it passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a>, which founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucia">Seleucia</a> ad Belum, Antigoneia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> on the river. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a> took it in 64 BCE for the new Roman province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>. The emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a> built the Lake Homs dam in 290 CE. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate</a> won the Battle of the Iron Bridge near Antioch in 637 CE and soon controlled the whole valley, and in the 12th century the river formed the boundary between the Principality of Antioch and that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The river has gone by several names. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrians</a> called it Arantu in the 9th century BCE and the Egyptians Araunti, and the name became Orontes in Greek. Macedonian settlers at Apamea called it the Axius, from which the Arabic name al-ʿĀṣī derives; the word also means insubordinate in Arabic, and a folk etymology ties it to the river's flow from south to north. The Roman satirist Juvenal, writing in the early 2nd century, used the Orontes as a metonym for eastern influence in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, saying that the Syrian Orontes had long flowed into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiber-river">Tiber</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Orontes River” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ebla lay at Tell Mardikh, about 55 kilometres south-west of Aleppo in northern Syria, on the rolling plain between the <b>Orontes</b> valley and the Euphrates.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ebla">Ebla · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Battle of Kadesh in 1274 BCE, fought against Ramesses II on the <b>Orontes River</b> under Muwatalli II, is often described as the largest chariot engagement of antiquity, with perhaps five thousand chariots involved on both sides.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hittite-empire">Hittite Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1178 Saladin granted Hama on the <b>Orontes</b> to his nephew Taqi al-Din Umar, whose descendants kept it as a hereditary principality within the Ayyubid family confederation.</blockquote><a href="/state/ayyubid-emirate-of-hama">Ayyubid Emirate of Hama</a></li><li><blockquote>The county ran along the coast from Jubail to Tortosa and inland to the <b>Orontes</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-tripoli">County of Tripoli</a></li><li><blockquote>Sampsigeramus I, an Arab chief, controlled the <b>Orontes</b> valley around Emesa, modern Homs, as the Seleucid Empire collapsed.</blockquote><a href="/state/emesene-dynasty">Emesene dynasty</a></li><li><blockquote>Hamath, modern Hama, rose after the Late Bronze Age collapse as a kingdom on the middle <b>Orontes</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/hamath">Hamath</a></li></ul></section>
